WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.120
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:05.154
<v Examiner>Describe the five steps of the nursing process,

3
00:00:05.154 --> 00:00:08.834
<v Examiner>and compare a medical diagnosis with a nursing diagnosis.

4
00:00:09.284 --> 00:00:15.073
The nursing process is an organised sequence of problem-solving steps used to identify

5
00:00:15.073 --> 00:00:19.112
and manage a patient's actual and potential health problems.

6
00:00:19.112 --> 00:00:24.699
It needs critical thinking, and it's at the core of holistic, patient-focused care.

7
00:00:24.699 --> 00:00:26.180
Five steps: A D P I E.

8
00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:27.386
Assessment.

9
00:00:27.386 --> 00:00:30.065
Observation, interview and examination.

10
00:00:30.065 --> 00:00:34.050
Objective data are observable and measurable facts: signs.

11
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:39.134
Subjective data are what only the patient can feel and describe: symptoms.

12
00:00:39.134 --> 00:00:41.333
Sources: primary is the patient.

13
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:45.661
Secondary is the family, records, test results and other staff.

14
00:00:45.661 --> 00:00:48.753
Tertiary is textbooks, journals and policies.

15
00:00:49.203 --> 00:00:49.906
Diagnosis.

16
00:00:49.906 --> 00:00:54.551
Identifying actual or potential problems that nursing can prevent,

17
00:00:54.551 --> 00:00:59.899
reduce or resolve on its own, written as N A N D A International statements,

18
00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:06.163
and prioritised: life-threatening first, then safety, then what the patient says matters.

19
00:01:06.613 --> 00:01:07.212
Planning.

20
00:01:07.212 --> 00:01:11.274
Prioritised short- and long-term goals with outcome criteria.

21
00:01:11.274 --> 00:01:16.735
The goal restates the diagnosis as improvement, control or absence of the problem,

22
00:01:16.735 --> 00:01:21.996
and it should be SMART: specific, measurable, achievable, realistic and timely.

23
00:01:21.996 --> 00:01:26.858
For example, walks fifty metres with assistance within forty-eight hours.

24
00:01:27.308 --> 00:01:28.261
Implementation.

25
00:01:28.261 --> 00:01:33.277
Interventions that are evidence-based, safe, within the legal scope of nursing,

26
00:01:33.277 --> 00:01:35.500
and compatible with medical orders.

27
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:36.745
Evaluation.

28
00:01:36.745 --> 00:01:39.996
Has the goal been met, partly met or not met?

29
00:01:39.996 --> 00:01:44.547
Each one is timed, dated and signed, and evaluation is ongoing.

30
00:01:44.997 --> 00:01:45.993
The comparison.

31
00:01:45.993 --> 00:01:51.107
A medical diagnosis names the patient's disease or condition, like pneumonia.

32
00:01:51.107 --> 00:01:56.884
A nursing diagnosis names the patient's actual or potential response to that condition,

33
00:01:56.884 --> 00:02:01.334
which the nurse can manage, like risk for infection, or acute pain.

34
00:02:01.334 --> 00:02:05.584
So the nursing diagnosis explains the effect of the medical one.

35
00:02:06.034 --> 00:02:12.244
Nursing process, A D P I E: assessment, with objective signs and subjective symptoms;

36
00:02:12.244 --> 00:02:18.745
diagnosis, N A N D A, prioritised; planning, SMART goals; implementation, evidence-based,

37
00:02:18.745 --> 00:02:24.370
safe, in scope; evaluation, met, partly met or not met, timed, dated, signed.

38
00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.000
Medical diagnosis names the disease;

39
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.237
nursing diagnosis names the response the nurse can manage.
